{
  "requirement": "Number of hexadecimal digits in the identity hash of an anonymous selector segment.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement states one obligation: `HASH_DIGITS` is a `usize` constant equal to 12, and it is the number of hex digits in the identity hash of an anonymous selector segment.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement and covers it in two ways:\n- It imports `trust_but_verify::outline::selector::HASH_DIGITS` and asserts it equals 12. This also checks the constant's public path and its use as a `usize` (it is compared against `prefix.len()`).\n- For each anonymous `line@…` selector, it asserts that the part after `line@` has exactly `HASH_DIGITS` characters and that all of them are hex digits. It also asserts that this part equals the first 12 characters of the item's `structural_hash`. A different digit count, a non-hex digit or a mismatch with the identity hash would fail.\n\nA grep for `HASH_DIGITS` shows one definition, in `outline/selector.rs#const[HASH_DIGITS]`. Anonymous segments in plain text (`outline/mod.rs#fn[plain_text]`), in tree-based outlines (`Builder::finish`) and in Markdown (`markdown.rs#fn[parse]`) all slice the hash with that constant. `parse_segment` checks parsed selectors against it. So the line-selector assertions exercise the same constant that every anonymous segment uses, and a separate per-language test of the digit count would add nothing.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` is a `{test}` for the public-API inventory, not for this value. Even so, it checks that the export is present.\n\nI found no gaps.",
  "references": "626b887ea4829745118e76e022ba528f90dd6869e94e42a75be65619ddaab2e1",
